  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nStrengthening upper ridging over the western US, coupled with a\nbroad surface high pressure over the eastern US, will lead to a \nreturn of above normal temperatures for the remainder of the \nweek and through the weekend. This will support highs climbing \ninto the 80s for many locations today. Tomorrow, a weak front \nwill move into the area and settle just south of the area \ntomorrow night. Ahead of this boundary, temperatures will surge \nin the mid to potentially upper 80s, especially in our southern \nzones. This boundary settles just to the south of the area as \nhigh pressure moves into the Great Lakes region Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. That boundary lifts northward Thursday and by \nthe evening and overnight, it still looks like there is some low\npotential for showers to develop along the nose of the low-\nlevel jet nosing into that boundary. The best forcing and \nascent looks shifted northwest into Nebraska though, so our \npotential has decreased. PoPs look low enough now to not carry \nany mention of corresponding weather but there is still a 5-10% \nchance for precipitation across northern Missouri.\n\nFor later in the week and into the weekend, broad ridging spreads \neastward with very warm mid-level temperatures moving east out of \nthe higher terrain of the West. With persistent warm advection,\nwe should see highs in the 80s be maintained. Our normal highs \nfor the end of the week are near 70 so we will likely be at \nleast 10 degrees above normal and potentially 15+ degrees above \nnormal by late in the weekend. We'll also need to monitor the \ntrack a potential tropical system. Models move a system onshore \nalong the Gulf coast and take it to the east of the area Sunday \ninto Monday. So for now, it looks dry for our forecast area. \nAdditionally, with the upper ridge shifting east into the middle\nof the country, deep troughing develops over the western US/ \neastern Pacific. This system may bring some unsettled weather \ninto the middle of the US next week, generally at the tail end \nof this forecast or beyond it.",
